  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA general troughing pattern will persist aloft through much of the \nperiod as a mid-level ridge shifts farther east, maintaining \npredominately southwesterly flow over South Texas. PWATs currently \nnear 1.4 inches will briefly fall below 1.0 inch overnight before \nrebounding to 1.75-1.90 inches Monday. Increasing moisture combined \nwith passing upper-level disturbances will support low to moderate \nrain/thunderstorm chances Monday through Thursday night. \n\nStorm total rainfall through Thursday night is generally expected to \naverage around 0.50 inch, although localized amounts approaching \n1.50 inches will be possible, particularly near the coast. Rain \nchances should gradually diminish heading into next weekend, \nalthough lingering moisture and disturbances aloft may support \nisolated showers. \n\nTemperatures will remain fairly consistent through the workweek, \nwith highs generally in the lower to middle 80s and overnight lows \nin the middle 60s to lower 70s. Warmer conditions are expected next \nweekend, with highs potentially returning to the 90s across portions \nof the Brush Country. \n\nMinor coastal flooding remains a concern through much of the week as \npersistent onshore flow, elevated swells, and astronomical tides \nassociated with the full moon that occurred Sunday night contribute \nto higher water levels. There's a Coastal Flood Advisory in effect \nfrom 20z Monday to 05z Tuesday.",
